      {
        "id": "p30-q1-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "After this step, what should she do, without expanding, to factorise the quadratic expression?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Use the difference of squares: $(x+2)^2-3^2=(x-1)(x+5)$.",
          "worked": "Recognise $9=3^2$ and use the difference of squares.\n$$\\begin{align*}(x+2)^2-9&=(x+2)^2-3^2\\\\(x+2)^2-9&=(x+2-3)(x+2+3)\\\\(x+2)^2-9&=(x-1)(x+5).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p30-q1-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Use this method to factorise $x^2+4x-7$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x+2-\\sqrt{11})(x+2+\\sqrt{11})$.\n\nComplete the square, then use the difference of squares.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x^2+4x-7&=(x+2)^2-4-7\\\\x^2+4x-7&=(x+2)^2-11\\\\x^2+4x-7&=(x+2)^2-(\\sqrt{11})^2\\\\x^2+4x-7&=(x+2-\\sqrt{11})(x+2+\\sqrt{11}).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      }
    ]
